To demonstrate, let's load the `NEMO/prg-env` module and then use the `module list`
command to show which modules we currently have loaded in our environment.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load NEMO/prg-env
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module list
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Currently Loaded Modules:
  1) slurm/23.02.1                  9) ncurses/6.4-GCCcore-12.3.0              17) gettext/0.21.1-GCCcore-12.3.0           25) gzip/1.12-GCCcore-12.3.0
  2) lmod                          10) libreadline/8.2-GCCcore-12.3.0          18) git/2.41.0-GCCcore-12.3.0-nodocs        26) lz4/1.9.4-GCCcore-12.3.0
  3) Anaconda3/2024.02-1           11) OpenSSL/1.1                             19) Szip/2.1.1-GCCcore-12.3.0               27) zstd/1.5.5-GCCcore-12.3.0
  4) GCCcore/12.3.0                12) Perl-bundle-CPAN/5.36.1-GCCcore-12.3.0  20) intel-compilers/2023.1.0                28) bzip2/1.0.8-GCCcore-12.3.0
  5) zlib/1.2.13-GCCcore-12.3.0    13) numactl/2.0.16-GCCcore-12.3.0           21) UCX/1.14.1-GCCcore-12.3.0               29) netCDF/4.9.2-iimpi-2023a
  6) binutils/2.40-GCCcore-12.3.0  14) cURL/8.0.1-GCCcore-12.3.0               22) impi/2021.9.0-intel-compilers-2023.1.0  30) netCDF-Fortran/4.6.1-iimpi-2023a
  7) Perl/5.36.1-GCCcore-12.3.0    15) XZ/5.4.2-GCCcore-12.3.0                 23) iimpi/2023a                             31) netCDF-C++4/4.3.1-iimpi-2023a
  8) expat/2.5.0-GCCcore-12.3.0    16) libxml2/2.11.4-GCCcore-12.3.0           24) HDF5/1.14.0-iimpi-2023a                 32) NEMO/prg-env
```
{: .output}

So in this case, loading the `NEMO/prg-env` module also loaded a variety of other
modules. Let's try unloading the `NEMO/prg-env` package.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module unload NEMO/prg-env
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module list
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Currently Loaded Modules:
  1) slurm/23.02.1                  9) ncurses/6.4-GCCcore-12.3.0              17) gettext/0.21.1-GCCcore-12.3.0           25) gzip/1.12-GCCcore-12.3.0
  2) lmod                          10) libreadline/8.2-GCCcore-12.3.0          18) git/2.41.0-GCCcore-12.3.0-nodocs        26) lz4/1.9.4-GCCcore-12.3.0
  3) Anaconda3/2024.02-1           11) OpenSSL/1.1                             19) Szip/2.1.1-GCCcore-12.3.0               27) zstd/1.5.5-GCCcore-12.3.0
  4) GCCcore/12.3.0                12) Perl-bundle-CPAN/5.36.1-GCCcore-12.3.0  20) intel-compilers/2023.1.0                28) bzip2/1.0.8-GCCcore-12.3.0
  5) zlib/1.2.13-GCCcore-12.3.0    13) numactl/2.0.16-GCCcore-12.3.0           21) UCX/1.14.1-GCCcore-12.3.0               29) netCDF/4.9.2-iimpi-2023a
  6) binutils/2.40-GCCcore-12.3.0  14) cURL/8.0.1-GCCcore-12.3.0               22) impi/2021.9.0-intel-compilers-2023.1.0  30) netCDF-Fortran/4.6.1-iimpi-2023a
  7) Perl/5.36.1-GCCcore-12.3.0    15) XZ/5.4.2-GCCcore-12.3.0                 23) iimpi/2023a                             31) netCDF-C++4/4.3.1-iimpi-2023a
  8) expat/2.5.0-GCCcore-12.3.0    16) libxml2/2.11.4-GCCcore-12.3.0           24) HDF5/1.14.0-iimpi-2023a
```
{: .output}

So using `module unload` "un-loads" a module along with its dependencies.
